  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM EST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nEarly this afternoon surface analysis showed a pair of 994mb lows to \nthe SE and NW of the U.P. One was over the eastern Great Lakes and \nthe other was moving into central Manitoba. The western U.P. was \nunder the saddle point (or col) with relatively weak flow (light \nwinds) while the eastern U.P. remained under northerly winds gusting \nas high as 25 mph. Temperatures had warmed into the 40s in the east \nbut were struggling to make it above freezing in the interior west. \nGOES visible satellite imagery showed a mix of high and low clouds \nacross the area; the low cloud deck was eroding over the western and \neastern U.P. resulting in breaks in the clouds. To out west, a band \nof clouds extended from the Manitoba low south through MN and into \nNE/KS along a frontal boundary, moving east.\n\nOverall the upper pattern will not change significantly through the \nupcoming week with a split jet coming into the west coast keeping \nthe storm track to the north and south of the U.P. Cold, arctic air \nwill be mostly bottled up over the northern Canadian provinces with \nperiodic colder air intrusions into the northern U.S. as a parade of \nshortwaves moves to our north. Widespread winter weather impacts are \nnot expected through the forecast period (ending Thursday).\n\nTonight the low over Manitoba will track through northern Ontario \ndragging a frontal boundary across the U.P. A band of light, \nscattered, precipitation will accompany the front. With warm air \naloft expecting any precipitation to fall as liquid. Precipitation \namounts are expected to be very light as it will be fighting against \nvery dry air in the low levels. However, where temperatures and \nsurfaces are below freezing, mainly the interior western/central \nU.P. a light glaze of ice could occur (30% chance). It only takes a \nsmall amount of ice to cause slippery surfaces, those that will be \nout late tonight and early Saturday should stay aware of the \nconditions and use caution should freezing rain occur.\n\nOn Saturday temperatures will be above average with highs reaching \ninto the 40s across much of the area. Light rain showers will be \npossible through the afternoon over the eastern U.P. but any \nrainfall will be light. A cold front will push through the area late \nSaturday night into Sunday morning with temperatures falling back \ninto the 30s over the central and west and near 40 in the east. A \nfew light lake effect rain showers are expected over the Keweenaw \nSaturday afternoon and evening. Thereafter more widespread lake \neffect showers develop, and transition to snow from west to east \nthrough on Sunday over the northwest wind snowbelts. Moisture will \nbe limited and doesn't look to extend very far into the DGZ, thus \nexpect snow accumulations to remain around an inch or less. Snow \nshowers will diminish in the west on Monday and linger into Monday \nevening over the east. Any minor travel impacts will be limited to \nthe northwest winds snowbelts of the western/central U.P. Sunday \nnight, daytime temperatures Monday warming to near 30 in the west \nand mid 30s east should keep most accumulations in the grassy areas, \nlimiting impacts to roadways.\n\nHigh temperatures then rebound starting Tuesday, 30s to near 40, and \nwarm further into the 40s for Tuesday through Thursday. Couldn't \nrule out a few light rain/snow showers Tuesday and Tuesday night as \na clipper moves north of the area but overall chances will be \nlimited (less than 30%).",
